Revised Unit Plan: AHCLPW313 - Undertake Sampling and Testing of Water

Course: Certificate III in Conservation and Ecosystem Management
Delivery Mode: Blended (Classroom, Field-Based Learning & App-Assisted)
Total Nominal Hours: 40
Sessions: 10 (4 hours each)

Key to App Integration: The app will serve as the central, interactive textbook, reference guide, and practice tool for the unit. It will be used for pre-reading, in-class activities, fieldwork support, and self-directed learning.

---

Session Plan Overview

Session 1: Introduction to Water Sampling and Testing

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Understand the importance of water quality testing.
  · Identify key legislative and workplace requirements.
  · Recognise different water sampling techniques.
· Activities:
  · App Activity (In-Class): Students navigate to Tab 0: Why test water? . Use the interactive tables and cards to facilitate a group discussion on the Australian context. The app provides all the content and prompts for the discussion.
  · Presentation & Video: The instructor can use the information in Tab 0 as a presentation guide. Students can watch the embedded videos (e.g., EPA Victoria's "How we measure water quality") through the app's Tab 7: Resources.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students are assigned to explore Tab 0 and Tab 1: Key Parameters to research local water bodies and their uses, using the app's information as a starting point.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "What are some key factors affecting water quality in our region? Use the 'Australia's unique challenges' section in the app to help you."
  · "Why do different industries have different water quality standards? Check the 'Compliance & regulations' card in Tab 0."

Session 2: Planning for Water Sampling

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Develop a water sampling plan.
  · Identify safe work procedures and risk assessments.
· Activities:
  · App Activity (In-Class): Students work in groups to complete a case study designing a water sampling plan. They will use the template provided in the app's Tab 7: Resources ("Downloadable resources & templates" -> "Sampling Plan").
  · Workshop: A hazard identification workshop based on Tab 3: Planning & Safety. Students can use the "Risk Assessment" template from the app's Tab 7: Resources to conduct a risk assessment for their local creek.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students are instructed to read the "Planning & Safety" section (Tab 3) to prepare for the field trip.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "What risks might be present when sampling in different environments? Use the 'Risk assessment essentials' in Tab 3 to help you."
  · "How can you ensure consistency in sampling techniques? Review the 'Quality assurance' guidelines in Tab 2."

Session 3: Field Techniques for Water Sampling

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Demonstrate correct water sampling techniques.
  · Follow chain of custody procedures.
· Activities:
  · Pre-Field Briefing: Use the app's Tab 3 and Tab 4 to review the equipment checklist and sample collection plan.
  · Field Trip: Students collect water samples. They will use their mobile devices or tablets to access the app in the field, specifically referencing Tab 2: Equipment & Methods and Tab 4: Samples & Dispatch for correct techniques.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students document field observations in a report. They can use the "Field Data Sheet" template from Tab 7: Resources.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "Why is it important to label and preserve samples correctly? Check the 'Preservation & storage' section in Tab 4."
  · "What factors can influence sample contamination? Review the 'Best practice' list in Tab 2."

Session 4: On-Site Water Testing Methods

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Conduct on-site testing for pH, temperature, and dissolved oxygen.
· Activities:
  · Practical Session: Students use handheld meters and field kits, guided by the equipment descriptions in Tab 2. They can use the Tab 8: Water Quality Simulator to predict how environmental conditions (like temperature and light) might affect their readings.
  · Small Group Discussion: Students interpret their real-time results using the "Normal Ranges" table in Tab 5: Interpreting Results.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students research common water pollutants and their effects using the information in Tab 1: Key Parameters.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "How do environmental conditions impact on-site test results? Use the simulator in Tab 8 to see the effect of temperature on Dissolved Oxygen."
  · "What limitations do field tests have compared to lab tests? Compare the information in Tab 2 (Field) and Tab 5 (Lab/Interpretation)."

Session 5: Laboratory Testing and Data Interpretation

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Understand laboratory analysis techniques.
  · Interpret water quality data.
· Activities:
  · Guest Speaker or Video: Use the resources in Tab 7: Resources to find relevant lab videos or links to Analytical Services Tasmania.
  · Data Analysis Workshop: Students compare their field results (from Tab 4's Data Sheet) with potential lab results. They use Tab 5: Interpreting Results and the "Trigger Levels" table in Tab 7 to analyze data.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students write a reflection on lab testing processes, using Tab 4: Samples & Dispatch (specifically the "Chain of Custody" section) as a reference.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "What are the advantages of lab testing over field testing? Compare the information in Tab 2 and Tab 5."
  · "How can data trends inform environmental management decisions? Use the 'Case study' in Tab 5 as an example."

Session 6: Assessing and Reporting Water Quality

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Compare test results to water quality guidelines.
  · Document findings in a report.
· Activities:
  · Group Activity: Students present findings from their field and lab tests. They can use the app to show their data and use the guidelines in Tab 0 and Tab 5 to justify their conclusions.
  · Report Writing Exercise: Students use the app's Tab 6: Test Your Knowledge and Tab 7: Resources as a reference for final assessments.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students review a government water quality report, using the app to understand the terminology and concepts.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "What challenges arise when interpreting water quality data? Refer to the 'Common issues & what they mean' and 'Diurnal and seasonal variation' sections in Tab 5."
  · "How can reporting influence environmental decision-making? Review the 'When to report urgently' checklist in Tab 5."

Session 7: Water Quality Management Strategies

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Identify water quality management strategies.
  · Understand remediation methods.
· Activities:
  · Case Study & Brainstorm: Students use the "Scenarios" in Tab 6: Test Your Knowledge to discuss pollution events and brainstorm management responses.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students research best practices for catchment management, using the app's resources (Tab 7) and the case study in Tab 5 as a foundation.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "What are some common causes of water contamination? Review the 'Key Parameters' in Tab 1 to understand what each pollutant indicates."
  · "How can land management practices improve water quality? Consider the findings from the 'Healthy' vs. 'Eutrophic' scenarios in the Tab 8 simulator."

Session 8: Advanced Water Testing Techniques

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Conduct advanced testing methods (turbidity, salinity, EC, BOD).
· Activities:
  · Hands-On Practical: Students use advanced sensors. They can reference the equipment guides in Tab 2: Equipment & Methods.
  · Data Comparison Exercise: Students use the Tab 8: Simulator to change parameters (e.g., salinity) to see how they affect other readings, replicating the field vs. lab comparison.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students research the role of macroinvertebrates in water quality assessment, using the resources linked in Tab 7.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "How do different pollutants impact water ecosystems? Use the 'Water Quality Simulator' in Tab 8 to see how high nutrients and temperature affect Dissolved Oxygen."
  · "What role does biodiversity play in water health monitoring? Relate this back to the 'Protect human & ecosystem health' section in Tab 0."

Session 9: Final Field Assessment

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Demonstrate proficiency in field sampling and testing.
  · Apply risk management strategies.
· Activities:
  · Independent Field Assessment: Students conduct a full water sampling procedure. They will use their mobile devices to access the app's Tab 4: Samples & Dispatch and Tab 3: Planning & Safety as a quick reference for procedures and checklists.
  · Peer Review: Students give feedback on techniques, using the best-practice guides in the app.
  · Self-Directed Learning: Students prepare a summary report of findings, using the app's "Field Data Sheet" and "Sampling Plan" as templates.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "What challenges did you encounter in the field? Could you use the app's troubleshooting tips to resolve them?"
  · "How would you improve your sampling approach next time?"

Session 10: Review and Assessment Submission

· Learning Outcomes:
  · Finalise and submit reports.
  · Reflect on learning outcomes.
· Activities:
  · App Activity (In-Class): Students complete the Tab 6: Test Your Knowledge quiz as a final, low-stakes self-assessment to solidify concepts.
  · Peer Presentations: Students present their water quality reports. They can use the app to show their data and decision-making process.
  · Final Assessment Submission: Students submit their final reports (which they've been building using the app's templates).
  · Group Discussion: Students reflect on their learning, using the app's "Your progress" bar and key takeaways from each section.
· Teacher Prompts:
  · "How has your understanding of water sampling changed? Reflect on your journey through the app's 9 tabs."
  · "What are some key takeaways from this unit? Use the 'Ready for assessment!' checklist in Tab 7 to guide your final thoughts."

---

Assessment Overview (Unchanged)

· Practical Demonstration (Field and Lab Sampling) – 40%
· Report Writing (Data Interpretation & Comparison) – 30%
· Knowledge Test (Legislation & Methods) – 20%
· Self-Reflection Assignment – 10%

How the App Supports Assessment:

1. Practical Demonstration: The app serves as the reference guide during fieldwork, ensuring students follow correct procedures and checklists.
2. Report Writing: The app provides the templates and data sheets that students can use to structure their reports.
3. Knowledge Test: The Tab 6: Quiz and the glossary in Tab 7 are the ideal study tools for this assessment.
4. Self-Reflection: The "Your progress" bars in the app, combined with the final review checklist, provide a clear structure for students to assess their own learning journey.
